<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Idia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1504">1504</a>–<a href="/years/1516">1516</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Queen Idia, Iyoba Idia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Idia was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esigie">Esigie</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> from 1504 to 1550, and the first Iyoba (queen mother) of Benin. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ozolua">Oba Ozolua</a> died she raised an army that defeated Esigie's brother Arhuaran, the heir by tradition, and secured the throne for her son; she was alive during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idah">Idah</a> war of 1515–1516, in which she helped Benin to victory. Esigie gave her the title of Iyoba with a palace of her own and ended the custom of killing the king's mother, and after her death he commissioned brass heads of her. Several images of her were looted in 1897, and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> mask of her in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> became the symbol of the FESTAC festival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> in 1977.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/idia.jpg" alt="Idia" width="330" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Memorial_bust_of_a_king's_mother_iyoba,_Nigeria,_Benin_Kingdom,_early_16th_century_AD,_gunmetal_bronze_-_Ethnological_Museum,_Berlin_-_DSC02225_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
